{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":281686,"duration_api_ms":280047,"num_turns":35,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"1611b9d6-8e0a-4ec0-abbd-73281c738c69","total_cost_usd":1.4736789999999995,"usage":{"input_tokens":29,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68054,"cache_read_input_tokens":1607443,"output_tokens":9779,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68054,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":29,"outputTokens":9779,"cacheReadInputTokens":1607443,"cacheCreationInputTokens":68054,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.4736789999999995,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In `crates/tank_core/src/systems/substrate.rs`, the `MICROFAUNA_O2_PER_MG_C_RESPIRED` constant (2.67) duplicates `O2_PER_MG_C_RESPIRED` in `microfauna.rs:7`. Extract a single shared constant (e.g., in a common module or `types/`) so the stoichiometric ratio cannot silently diverge between the two systems.","rationale":"Both constants represent the same physical value (32/12 ≈ 2.67 mg O₂ per mg C respired). Independent copies risk one being updated without the other, producing inconsistent O₂ accounting between the actual microfauna respiration pathway and the substrate penetration model."},{"body":"In `crates/tank_core/src/systems/substrate.rs:188-191`, the hardcoded `0.01` daily detritus consumption fraction mirrors the same magic number in `microfauna.rs:73`. Extract a named constant (e.g., `MICROFAUNA_DETRITUS_DAILY_FRACTION`) or reuse the one from `microfauna.rs` so the substrate O₂ demand estimate stays synchronized with the actual microfauna consumption rate.","rationale":"The substrate estimate of microfauna O₂ demand must track the actual microfauna system's consumption rates to produce a physically consistent penetration depth. Duplicated magic numbers risk divergence when the microfauna system's detritus processing rate is tuned."},{"body":"In `crates/tank_core/src/systems/substrate.rs:159-178`, the per-habitat accessibility factors (GlassHardscape=1.0, SubstrateSurface=substrate_surface_access, PlantSurfaces=0.7, FilterMedia=0.15, SubstrateDeep=0.0) are duplicated from `microfauna.rs:157-164` (`microfauna_periphyton_accessibility`). Factor these into a shared function or call the existing `microfauna_periphyton_accessibility` to prevent the two implementations from drifting.","rationale":"The substrate O₂ demand estimate's accuracy depends on correctly mirroring how the microfauna system distributes periphyton grazing across habitats. The current inline match duplicates the authoritative accessibility table and will silently produce incorrect penetration depths if only one copy is updated."},{"body":"The `effective_substrate_porosity` change (substrate.rs:133-140) now uses only the top layer's porosity for the entire Bouldin diffusion calculation instead of the depth-weighted average. When penetration depth exceeds the top layer's thickness, the model underestimates diffusion through the higher-porosity deeper layer. Add a comment documenting this as a known conservative simplification, or extend the model to switch porosity at layer boundaries.","rationale":"For heterogeneous stacks where a thin cap (e.g., 1 cm sand) sits atop a thick coarse layer (e.g., 5 cm), the surface-only porosity significantly underestimates actual penetration depth. The new test validates the direction-of-effect but the magnitude error could grow large for thin-cap scenarios. At minimum, documenting this as a deliberate conservatism prevents future confusion about model accuracy."}],"summary":"The diff implements two changes: (1) decomposer startup scaling now uses volume instead of footprint (while nitrifiers keep footprint scaling), and (2) substrate O₂ penetration now accounts for microfauna respiration and uses surface-layer-only porosity. Both changes are well-motivated and all tests pass. The main concerns are maintainability risks from duplicated constants and logic between the substrate O₂ demand estimator and the microfauna system — specifically the O₂/C stoichiometric constant, the 0.01 detritus consumption fraction, and the per-habitat accessibility factors are all copy-pasted between the two modules and could silently diverge. The effective_substrate_porosity simplification is physically reasonable but should be documented as a conservative approximation for heterogeneous stacks."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"0eb4fca2-8455-4efe-9986-5564c30a9dae"}
